  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Sunday)\nIssued at 933 PM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nExtended range model guidance suggests that a weak low-level ridge \nwill build northeastward into the local forecast area on Thursday,\nin the wake of a departing surface trough and axis of \nrain/thunderstorms. This (along with unamplified/westerly flow \naloft of 35-45 knots) will support dry conditions, with overnight \nlows falling into the l-m 50s Thursday morning, followed by \nafternoon highs in the l-m 70s. On Thursday night, large scale \nascent will strengthen considerably as a mid-level wave digs east-\nsoutheastward into the TN Valley in the flow around an upper low \nacross the Great Lakes. Although the tropospheric column will be \nfar too dry to support any risk for precipitation, a minor \nincrease in cloud cover aloft should accompany the passage of this\nsystem. At the surface, a reinforcing cold front will spread \nsoutheastward in conjunction with the shortwave trough, with light\nnortherly winds in the low-levels (beneath NW flow aloft) \nexpected to result in a continuation of dry weather from Friday- \nSaturday night. Early morning lows in the m30s-l40s can be \nexpected on both Saturday/Sunday, with afternoon highs in the l-m \n60s. Low chances for rain may return to parts of the region by \nSunday afternoon, either as a result of a weakening clipper system\ndiving into the Mid-MS Valley or a lower- latitude disturbance \nshifting eastward along the northwest Gulf Coast. However, due to \nvastly different model solutions at this time range, we will keep \nPOPs in the 10-20% range.",
